  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals mostly cloudy skies \noverhead Upper Michigan behind a departing shortwave. As troughing \nlingers and a weak vort max pivots over the area this afternoon, \nCAM guidance suggests that showers and thunderstorms may redevelop \nacross the central and eastern half. There remains some uncertainty \nabout exact timing and spatial extent of storms today given weak \nforcing, varying degrees of capping from west to east, and marginal \nbut slightly increasing shear. Should thunderstorms manage to root \nthemselves to a boundary and tap into enough of a shear/cape combo, \ncant rule out some small hail and gusty winds. Otherwise, temps fall \ninto the 50s/60s with patchy dense fog is possible under calming \nwinds and increase subsidence tonight.\n\nAdditional rounds of showers and storms are likely through the rest \nof the weekend into the early parts of next week as a \"Ring of Fire\" \npattern sets up across the CONUS. Parked over the southern and \ncentral Plains is a stout ridge throwing weak shortwave \nperturbations along its northern fringes into the Great Lakes. This \ntype of setup gives us high confidence that showers and storms are \npossible in the area, but low confidence on spatial coverage, exact \ntiming, and to some extent the degree of severity. The next round \npresents itself tomorrow afternoon/evening although cant rule out an \nearly day storm. Latest CAM ensembles suggests the greatest \ninstability axis remains further west and south of the UP. Ensemble \n>40 dbz paintballs give a low confidence scenario on when and where \nthe greatest potential for strong to severe thunderstorms will \noccur. Afternoon hodographs show plenty of low-level streamwiseness \nacross the central UP tomorrow afternoon/evening, so cant rule out \nsome spinning storms capable of producing large hail and weak \ntornadoes. SPC has increased the severe risk to a Slight (level 2 of \n5) for the western 2/3rds of the UP.\n\nA more amplified ridge-riding shortwave dives south into the \nGreat Lakes on Monday, likely bringing a potential third round of \nstrong to severe storms to the area. Favorable kinematics look more \nreadily available in forecast soundings, though the main concern \nremains to be the timing of this wave. A faster, more progressive \nwave that works itself into the UP in the morning to early afternoon \nmay limit the severe potential this far north. SPC continues to \nmaintain a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5) for severe weather across the \nUP and all of downstate.\n\nQuieter weather returns beyond Monday into the middle of the week \nunder the ridge axis and building surface high pressure.",
